2,3-dimethylphenylamine can be prepared from 1,2-dimethylbenzene in two steps as shown.

{{figure:pdf_visual_01.png}}

Step 1 is catalysed by $\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4$.

The position of substitution in the electrophilic substitution of arenes can be explained based on the stability of the intermediate cations formed in the first step. The example given involves the bromination of methylbenzene.

{{figure:pdf_visual_04.png}}

Use this information and your knowledge about the stability of cations to suggest why the $\text{CH}_3$ group directs incoming electrophiles to the 2- and 4-positions in preference to the 3-position.
